2 & 9 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366344 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186366344 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366346 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}\\in R" "x(t)={c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{2}\\in R"] [:db/cas 17592186366347 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [:db/cas 17592186366348 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [:db/cas 17592186366349 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{2}\\in R" "x(t)={c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{2}\\in R"] [:db/cas 17592186366350 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [:db/cas 17592186366358 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}10x+6y \\\\ 25x+15y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}10x+6y \\\\ 25x+15y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186366358 :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot k+6\\cdot 5 \\\\ 25\\cdot k+15\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10k+30 \\\\ 25k+75\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10k+30=0 \\\\ 25k+75=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-3.$" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10\\cdot k+6\\cdot 5 \\\\ 25\\cdot k+15\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10k+30 \\\\ 25k+75\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10k+30=0 \\\\ 25k+75=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366365 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186366365 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366372 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366372 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(0,1))=4\\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\nNow, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(-1,0))=4\\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(0,1))=4\\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\nNow, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(-1,0))=4\\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186366379 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366379 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366386 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366386 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366399 :question/problem "![](images/24/2489bb3ecac186e81ef421fdb90047e7a3d6a1ad5985bb0a8155857dca709996.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "![](images/24/2489bb3ecac186e81ef421fdb90047e7a3d6a1ad5985bb0a8155857dca709996.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366399 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/358dcedefd029cdbd07f3c64b1264e568bfbac134b92a607e9d55c632c277b13.png)" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/358dcedefd029cdbd07f3c64b1264e568bfbac134b92a607e9d55c632c277b13.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186366420 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x-z \\\\ x+y \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}x-z \\\\ x+y \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366420 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{4},$ $b=-\\frac{1}{4},$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+1=1.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{4},$ $b=-\\frac{1}{4},$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+1=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366441 :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2z \\\\ w \\\\ z \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+w{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2z \\\\ w \\\\ z \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}=z{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+w{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366448 :question/worked-solution "Solving $2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=2y.$ Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Solving $2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=2y.$ Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix}=y{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366455 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=z{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366476 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366476 :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366478 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366479 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366481 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366482 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366483 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."] [:db/cas 17592186366497 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366497 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366504 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366506 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366507 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366508 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366509 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366510 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366511 :question/problem "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" "$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186366511 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,D-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nD-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(D-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $D$ are $\\lambda =-1$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$), $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "First, we find the matrix $\\,D-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nD-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(D-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $D$ are $\\lambda =-1$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$), $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366518 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366518 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,R-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nR-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(R-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st column, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4)+2(-2\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4)+(-4\\lambda +14) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,R-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nR-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(R-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st column, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4)+2(-2\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4)+(-4\\lambda +14) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366525 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=5.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366553 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186366553 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366560 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186366560 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366582 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366583 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366584 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366585 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366586 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366587 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186366587 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-3t+{t}^{2}) & =2-3t-5{t}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}t+(-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+1=3.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-3t+{t}^{2}) & =2-3t-5{t}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}t+(-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+1=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366594 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3{t}^{3}+3t+18,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9,\\,\\,q(t)=-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}=$" "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3{t}^{3}+3t+18,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9,\\,\\,q(t)=-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186366594 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t)+{x}_{2}(3{t}^{3}+3t+18)+{x}_{3}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){t}^{3}+(3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})t+(18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}↔{R}_{4} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-47 \\\\ -17{x}_{3}=85.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n9{x}_{2}+4\\cdot (-5)=-47\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3\\cdot (-3)+(-5)=-15\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=1.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t)+{x}_{2}(3{t}^{3}+3t+18)+{x}_{3}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){t}^{3}+(3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})t+(18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{3}↔{R}_{4} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-47 \\\\ -17{x}_{3}=85.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n9{x}_{2}+4\\cdot (-5)=-47\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3\\cdot (-3)+(-5)=-15\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366601 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-6{t}^{3}+6t+12,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,\\,q(t)=-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-6{t}^{3}+6t+12,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,\\,q(t)=-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186366601 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-6{t}^{3}+6t+12)+{x}_{2}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2)+{x}_{3}(-3{t}^{2}+2t) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18 \\\\ (-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{3}+(-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})t+(12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=19 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}-3\\cdot 2=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+(-3)=-9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{2}=-3.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-6{t}^{3}+6t+12)+{x}_{2}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2)+{x}_{3}(-3{t}^{2}+2t) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18 \\\\ (-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{3}+(-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})t+(12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=19 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}-3\\cdot 2=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+(-3)=-9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and therefore ${x}_{2}=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366615 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366615 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$" "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366622 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366622 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+6\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1})-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25-25)-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25)-75-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-63 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2} & =63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+5, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-7{z}_{2}^{2} & =63 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{21})^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{21}$ and $3.$" "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+6\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}+30{y}_{1}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1})-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25-25)-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{1}+25)-75-7{y}_{2}^{2}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2}-63 & =0 \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+5)^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2} & =63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+5, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-7{z}_{2}^{2} & =63 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{21})^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{21}$ and $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366629 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366629 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1})-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1)-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-2\\cdot 1-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4{y}_{2}+6 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2{z}_{1}^{2} & =4{z}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & =2{z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis." "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1})-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1)-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-2\\cdot 1-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4{y}_{2}+6 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2{z}_{1}^{2} & =4{z}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & =2{z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186366670 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366670 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of 