 we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366679 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366680 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366681 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{al