2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366697 :question/problem "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186366697 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=2,$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=2,$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366739 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366752 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186366759 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186366766 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186366794 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=1$\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=1$\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366822 :question/problem "Let\n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ z \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ z+1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ z \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf $r\\cdot s=1$, find the value of $z.$" "Let\n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ z \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ z+1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ z \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $r\\cdot s=1$, find the value of $z.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366945 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186366945 :question/worked-solution "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366974 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366975 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366976 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366977 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186366978 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367000 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367001 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367002 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367003 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367004 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186367005 :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367005 :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA+I∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A+I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A+I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A+I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A+I)v=0.$   \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A+I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A+I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA+I∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatri